WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.577
Idea. Bacteria in food grow in four phases. In this made-up example graph: first the lag phase, when they are making enzymes, not dividing yet. Then the exponential phase: numbers double again and again. Then the stationary phase: food runs short and waste builds up, so numbers stay level. Last, the death phase: numbers fall.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:38.922
Bacteria multiply fastest when they have three things: food, moisture, which means water, and warmth near their optimum temperature. The optimum is the temperature where their enzymes work best. Food preservation takes away one of these.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:56.333
Method. Step one: name the condition, such as temperature, moisture, food, or air. Step two: say what it does to the bacteria's enzymes, or to how fast they divide. Step three: state the result, so the food spoils faster or slower.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:17.247
Most bacteria in food grow fastest near body temperature, 37 degrees Celsius. Cold slows bacteria. A fridge, at about 4 degrees Celsius, slows their enzymes, so they multiply slowly. It does not kill them. Freezing stops growth for longer, but bacteria grow again after thawing.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:37.499
Heat kills. Boiling, pasteurising, which means heating milk to about 72 degrees Celsius for a short time, and thorough cooking denature the bacteria's enzymes: their shape changes, so they stop working. Most bacteria die. Sealing or vacuum packing keeps air and new microbes out.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:57.686
Step four, for a graph: different groups of bacteria have different optimum temperatures. In this made-up example, psychrophiles like cold. Mesophiles grow best near body temperature. Thermophiles grow best at about 50 to 70 degrees Celsius. Read the named group's optimum, and compare.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:11.961
Worked example. Now a real exam question. Bacteria multiply rapidly in food that is not preserved. Use words from the box to complete the passage. There are six blanks and six marks.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:24.595
The passage follows the curve. Blank one: the first phase, when bacteria are not dividing. That is lag. Blank two: bacteria multiplying rapidly. That is exponential.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:41.962
Blank three: nutrients run out, and numbers stay level. So dying cells are equal to dividing cells. Blank four: the last phase is death, the word in the box. In the death phase, numbers fall. So dividing cells are less than the number of dying cells.

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:54.388
Here is the full answer. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme. Notice that stationary is not used: the third stage is described, not named.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:07.861
Common mistake. A common mistake is to say a fridge kills bacteria. Cold only slows them down. Another is to call the not-dividing phase stationary. That is the lag phase.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:23.350
Also, do not put more than in the death phase. Dividing cells are fewer than dying cells. And do not assume every bacterium dies at 65 degrees Celsius: thermophiles grow well there. Freezing is not heat treatment.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:47.265
Your turn. On a camping trip, cooked rice is kept either in a hot car at 35 degrees Celsius, or in a cool box at 5 degrees Celsius. Explain why the rice in the car spoils faster. A friend says the cool box kills the bacteria: explain why they are wrong. Then explain why dried fruit keeps for months. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:04:08.471
Answer. Part a: 35 degrees Celsius is close to the optimum temperature of most bacteria in food, so their enzymes work fast and they multiply quickly. Part b: at 5 degrees Celsius, their enzymes are only slowed. The bacteria are still alive and grow again when the rice warms up.

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:18.413
Part c: drying removes water. Bacteria need moisture to grow, so in dried fruit they cannot multiply, and the fruit keeps for months.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:30.746
So, every time: name the condition, say what it does to the enzymes or to division, then state the result. For a word box, check that the comparison is true for that phase.
